<BaseLayout title="Research — Huanyu Chen" description="Research of Huanyu Chen: computational mechanics and interpretable physical AI.">
  <h1>Research</h1>
  <p>
    AI is learning to predict and act in the physical world, and robots are increasingly trained in simulation.
    Physics-based simulators are interpretable but hand-built; learned models are general but opaque. I build
    <strong>interpretable physical AI</strong>: models whose parts are written in the language of physics, so physics
    becomes the interpretation of what a model learns rather than a limit on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A common thread is <strong>connections between descriptions</strong>: how a 3D body becomes a rod or a shell, how a
    material law summarizes a microstructure, and how measurements become a model. Each one can keep all the physics if
    it is built right, and each one is a place where learning can enter in a form we can still read and check.
  </p>

